I cannot help with secretly recording conversations or monitoring other people’s communications without their knowledge.
That would enable covert surveillance and could violate privacy, consent, and wiretapping laws.
Safer, lawful alternatives include:
asking for explicit consent before recording;
using shared meeting notes or transcription with everyone informed;
documenting concerns through written summaries, timestamps, and saved messages you are already authorized to access;
using workplace or family monitoring tools only with clear notice, consent, and compliance with applicable law and policy.
If your goal is protection rather than surveillance, I can help you draft a consent notice, a recording policy, or a lawful evidence-preservation plan.
